implementationPlan.description:
TESTS FIRST (red). Load test-driven-development.

Prerequisites: task-2f2484b4 and task-2cf1c6be are DONE (dependsOn). Both edit the same /health handler in index.ts, so read their landed bytes first. Also read:
- the governor's comment on this row;
- mem:decision-daemon-health-host-rule (this row's design);
- docs/design/protected-profile-threat-model.md ('Found while deciding': /health).

Create packages/moe-daemon/src/server/healthHost.test.ts, using vitest and exact toBe/toEqual.

A. A pure table for isAllowedHealthHost(host, localAddress), imported from './healthHost.js'.

Allowed (true):
- host undefined (no Host header), with localAddress '127.0.0.1' and with undefined;
- 'localhost', 'localhost:9876' and 'LocalHost:1';
- '127.0.0.1', '127.0.0.1:9876' and '127.255.0.9:80' (all of 127/8);
- '[::1]', '[::1]:9876' and '[0:0:0:0:0:0:0:1]:9876';
- the WSL gateway case the governor asked for: '172.26.208.1:9876' and '172.26.208.1', with localAddress '172.26.208.1' and with '::ffff:172.26.208.1' (a dual-stack socket);
- an explicit IPv6 bind: '[fd00::5]:9876' with localAddress 'fd00::5'.

Refused (false):
- names:
  - 'evil.example' and 'evil.example:9876';
  - 'localhost.evil.example' and '127.0.0.1.nip.io:9876';
  - 'localhost.', 'sub.localhost' and 'LOCALHOST.:9876';
- IP literals that are neither loopback nor the connection's own address:
  - '10.0.0.5:9876' with localAddress '127.0.0.1';
  - '172.26.208.1:9876' with localAddress '127.0.0.1';
  - '[fd00::5]' with localAddress '::1';
  - '10.0.0.5' with localAddress undefined;
- malformed:
  - '' and ' ';
  - '127.0.0.1:', '127.0.0.1:abc', '127.0.0.1:0' and '127.0.0.1:65536';
  - 'user@127.0.0.1' and '127.0.0.1/x';
  - '::1' (unbracketed), '[::1' and '[::1]x';
  - '127.1', '0x7f.0.0.1' and '2130706433';
  - '[fe80::1%eth0]'.

B. The real handler. One describe spawns the REAL `_run` with `--host 127.0.0.1` on a free port. Spawn it through the tsx CLI with an isolated env (MOE_* stripped; HOME, USERPROFILE and TEMP redirected), the way delivery/acceptance.test.ts startDaemonChild does, including its fixture project setup and its daemon.json-plus-/health readiness wait. task-2f2484b4's bindHosts.test.ts spawned rows are the newer example. Never use port 9876.

Raw requests to 127.0.0.1:<port>:
- Host '127.0.0.1:<port>', Host 'localhost:<port>', and an absent Host (a raw 'GET /health HTTP/1.0\r\n\r\n' over net.connect) each answer 200. The JSON still carries status 'healthy', projectPath, pid, version, uptime, stats and checks. Assert those keys as a subset, so the row does not depend on the additive bindHost field.
- Host 'evil.example:<port>', '10.0.0.5:<port>' and 'localhost.evil.example' each answer 403, Content-Type text/plain, body exactly 'Forbidden': no JSON, no pid, no projectPath.
- The exported probeDaemonHealth(info, projectPath) still resolves true for the spawned daemon (the daemon CLI probe).
- GET /other still answers 426.

error handling in tests:
- every request and socket has a bounded timeout and is destroyed on it;
- the spawned daemon is killed in afterAll even when a row fails;
- every wait is bounded.

Red proof: on the pristine sources, `npx vitest run src/server/healthHost.test.ts` fails. Record:
- the import error from the missing module;
- with the table temporarily skipped, the 403 rows answering 200.

ADVERSARIAL SELF-REVIEW, once, as the final step. Load adversarial-self-review. Read the diff as a web page that controls a DNS name, and as an unlucky WSL or Docker user.

- Can any name reach the JSON? Check:
  - case variants;
  - a trailing dot;
  - '*.localhost';
  - IDN or punycode;
  - percent-encoding;
  - userinfo;
  - whitespace;
  - two Host headers (Node keeps the first);
  - an absolute-form request target such as 'GET http://evil/health'. req.url then is not '/health', so the request gets 426; confirm it.
- Can an IP Host that is neither loopback nor the arrival address pass? Check the IPv4-mapped, zone-id and non-canonical IPv6 forms.
- Is every legitimate client still answered: the CLI probe, doctor, JetBrains DaemonIdentity, the local-runner probe, the Docker HEALTHCHECK on localhost, and the launcher and proxy over the WSL gateway?
- Does the proxy classify a 403 as 'unknown' and so keep connecting?
- Is the guard the first statement, so a refused request costs nothing? Is no Host echoed or logged?
- Rails:
  - tests are present;
  - no MCP contract change;
  - .moe state stays backward-compatible;
  - no .moe edits and no Git writes;
  - none of the forbidden patterns;
  - no exploit walkthrough in comments, docs or summaries.
- Would I be embarrassed if this shipped?

Fix any finding and re-run step 5's gate fresh. Then overwrite the Serena memory 'task-task-80930ce6f0b54f7996e2c7eb7c835107-handoff' with the landed rule and names, for CQ-RF07's docs sweep and review.

comments.content:
Planner reply to @governor-693b7073 (architect-e4b7eee0, 2026-10-30). Plan rev 1 is approved.

Rule: GET /health answers its existing 200/503 JSON only for one of these:
- no Host header;
- `localhost`;
- a loopback IP literal;
- the IP literal of the address the connection arrived on (req.socket.localAddress, with IPv4-mapped normalized).
Anything else gets 403 text/plain with no JSON.

I did not take 'any IP literal', because DoD 1 asks for a 403 on an IP that is neither loopback nor the daemon's own address. Your WSL case still works: in NAT mode the gateway IP the agents use is the Windows vEthernet address the connection arrives on.

The tests include a gateway-IP Host row and a dual-stack row. The 200/503 + projectPath contract is unchanged for allowed Hosts, and the proxy's daemonIdentity classifies a 403 as 'unknown'.

Sequencing: index.ts is shared, so this row now dependsOn task-2f2484b4 and task-2cf1c6be. CQ-RF07 (task-f3f364a6) now dependsOn this row.

acceptanceCriteria:
The isAllowedHealthHost table passes:
- allowed: absent, localhost, 127/8, ::1, and the WSL gateway IP equal to the local address (also on a dual-stack socket);
- refused: names, a foreign IP, and malformed values.

compatibility:
Existing /health clients keep their 200/503 + projectPath contract:
- the daemon CLI probe, doctor, JetBrains DaemonIdentity and the local runner send 127.0.0.1;
- the Docker HEALTHCHECK sends localhost;
- the launcher WSL probe and the proxy send the gateway IP, the connection's own local address.

